es":{"color":"#ffffff","size":"10px"},"insert":"The above charts show the distribution of page performance, sorted by most-popular, not by EPT. Good performance analysis should focus on fixing what is most relevant to the business, and what can benefit the most number of users. While a larger segment of the chart by indicate worse performance, if it is further down the list (clockwise), it should be less of a priority. Blank \"Page App\" generally refers to Client apps, such as the standard Salesforce Mobile app."},{"attributes":{"align":"left"},"insert":"\n"}]},"interactions":[],"showActionMenu":true},"type":"text"},"text_19":{"parameters":{"content":{"richTextContent":[{"attributes":{"color":"#ffffff","size":"10px"},"insert":"There is a general best practice that considers 3 seconds to be a maximum, acceptable time for a page to load.  The chart above will respond to selections made elsewhere on the dashboard, in order to help pin-point performance degraded or improvd at a point in time.  Note, the Distribution charts below don't respond to these selections based on the dynamic nature of bucketing EPTs."},{"attributes":{"align":"left"},"insert":"\n"}]},"interactions":[],"showActionMenu":true},"type":"text"},"text_14":{"parameters":{"content":{"richTextContent":[{"attributes":{"size":"12px","color":"#ffffff"},"insert":"The chart on the right visualizes Apex runtime components (CPU, Execution, DB, Callout). Spikes in total runtime should correlate with a spike in one of these components, indicating a specific performance bottleneck (e.g., DB time spike suggests SOQL issues). Click on anomalies to investigate the slowest methods below."},{"attributes":{"align":"left"},"insert":"\n"}]},"interactions":[],"showActionMenu":true,"tooltip":""},"type":"text"},"text_13":{"parameters":{"content":{"richTextContent":[{"attributes":{"color":"#ffffff","size":"20px"},"insert":"API Calls Per Client"},{"attributes":{"align":"left"},"insert":"\n"}]},"interactions":[],"showActionMenu"